corlogic.known - rationale and evidence
=======================================

Why each mute entry exists, with enough detail to judge it. One section per
entry, headed by the entry and by the file it lives in when that is not
corlogic.known.


ERROR|1365v3047|*|*
-------------------

MDEV-37939, "Use correct error for invalid logarithm arguments". A logarithm
given a non-positive argument reports differently:

  12.3 and older   ERROR 1365: Division by 0
  13.0 and newer   ERROR 3047: Invalid argument error: <arg> in function log2.

13.x is the correct side. In /test/13.1/sql/item_func.cc the val_real methods
of Item_func_ln, _log, _log2 and _log10 call signal_log_domain_error(), which
raises ER_STD_INVALID_ARGUMENT; in /test/12.3 the same four call
signal_divide_by_null(). The change is owned by
/test/13.1/mysql-test/main/func_math_div_zero.test, present from 13.0.

Only INSERT, UPDATE and REPLACE see it as an error. The condition is a warning
and strict mode escalates it for those statements alone.

Scale: run /data/8469494 (CS 12.3.3 against CS 13.1.0, 100k trials, 210.8M
statements) gave 443 diffs and 308 reports. This one change is 315 of the diffs
and 307 of the reports, differing only in which log function and which DML
wrapped it. All 307 were checked one by one: nothing in any difference block but
the 1365 to 3047 swap, the named argument always zero or negative, plans
identical on both sides.


RESULT|content|*|ANALYZE TABLE*
-------------------------------

ANALYZE TABLE reports its own progress differently per version. The in-stream
statistics refresh is compared on state alone, but a generated ANALYZE inside a
testcase can still land here.


PLAN|ALL>index|*|*
------------------

A plan differing only in the access type on a table with no index. The optimizer
is choosing between two equal-cost paths.


RESULT|coltype|*CSINTRO*|*   in corlogic.known.mysql
----------------------------------------------------

The utf8 alias split. 13.1 dropped UTF8_IS_UTF8MB3 from the old_mode default
(/test/13.1/sql/sys_vars.cc, old_mode_names[], "deprecated since 13.1"), so a
bare _utf8 introducer means utf8mb4 there and utf8mb3 on 12.3 and older. This is
not a CONCAT bug:

  statement                              12.3                    13.1
  -------------------------------------  ----------------------  ----------------------
  CONCAT(_utf8 'h',' ',_binary 'w')      varbinary(3)            varchar(3) utf8mb4_bin
  CONCAT(_utf8mb3 'h',' ',_binary 'w')   varbinary(3)            varbinary(3)
  CONCAT(_utf8mb4 'h',' ',_binary 'w')   varchar(3) utf8mb4_bin  varchar(3) utf8mb4_bin

Same aggregation rule on both sides, and @@old_mode reads UTF8_IS_UTF8MB3 on
12.3 and empty on 13.1. Only the meaning of _utf8 moved.

MariaDB sides are fixed in code, not muted: at pre-flight corlogic strips
UTF8_IS_UTF8MB3 from each side's @@old_mode and, when every side then agrees,
emits one SET SESSION old_mode. Verified 12.3.3 against 13.1.0, one candidate
before and none after.

MySQL has no old_mode, so a cross-vendor run cannot be aligned and is muted
instead. Measured against 13.1.0: 5.5.62, 5.6.51 and 5.7.44 differ, 8.0.36 and
9.1.0 agree, so utf8 moved somewhere in the 8.0 series. The live UID on 5.7.44:

  RESULT|coltype|CSINTRO|SELECT CONCAT(_utf8 'X', 'Y', _binary 'Z')

The CSINTRO token comes from constructs_of and also covers the latin1 and ucs2
spellings, so every other column-type difference stays live. A mute beats a
VERSION_GATED row here, because the statement still runs on both sides so a
crash or a wrong row in it is still caught, where a gate drops the shape from
the stream entirely.


AFFECTED|0v1|UPDATE|UPDATE tX SET cX=DEFAULT   in corlogic.known.engines
------------------------------------------------------------------------

MDEV-40874, filed. An UPDATE writing a column back to the value it already holds
counts one affected row on Aria and none on InnoDB.

  CREATE TABLE t (c DECIMAL(8,3));
  INSERT INTO t VALUES(NULL);
  UPDATE t SET c=DEFAULT;

  InnoDB   affected=0
  Aria     affected=1

Reproduces on all 20 MariaDB builds in the sweep, none clean. Across the engine
axis RocksDB counts as Aria does, and MyISAM, MEMORY and ARCHIVE count as InnoDB
does. The pattern carries the normalised tX and cX the current code emits, where
the report predates that change and its own UID line reads t3 and c3.

Only this statement shape is muted, so the same defect reached another way is
reported again. That is wanted: a new data point on a filed bug, not a new bug.
Such a report carries the near-match notice naming this entry and MDEV-40874,
so it goes on that ticket. Measured on 13.1.0, InnoDB against Aria:

  UPDATE t1 SET c1=DEFAULT   muted, no report
  UPDATE t1 SET c1=c1        reported, notice names MDEV-40874


Reading a report before adding an entry
=======================================

A wide Bug Detection Matrix is usually not a second bug. Older versions lack the
function the testcase uses and answer "function does not exist", and the sweep
records only that a diff was seen, not which one. In /data/8469494 every report
flagging 26 of 30 builds used MONTHS_BETWEEN, which needs 12.3, and every report
flagging 16 of 30 used CRC32C, which 10.6 does not have. Read the error text
first.

Dropped candidates are not mute material either, being timing and row-order
effects. A server error whose cause is timing (1205, 1213, 1317, 1614, 1615,
1969, 3024) is now a timing note and is not compared at all, so the 16 drops in
/data/8469494 would be 5 today.

4609a4ba3fe6//RESULT|rows0v26|IN,SUBQ|SELECT * FROM tX o WHERE o.cX NOT IN (SELECT cX FROM tX) LIMIT N
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MDEV-40877, "Wrong result from NOT IN with a NULL in a materialized subquery".
MariaDB is the incorrect side. A NULL in the subquery result makes every
comparison UNKNOWN, so NOT IN can never be TRUE and no row qualifies. MariaDB
returns the rows whose value lies outside the range of the subquery column's
type; MySQL 5.5 through 9.1 all return none.

  CREATE TABLE t1 (c1 TINYINT);
  INSERT INTO t1 VALUES(21),(NULL);
  CREATE TABLE t2 (c1 INT);
  INSERT INTO t2 VALUES(1),(2),(3),(9999);
  SELECT * FROM t2 WHERE c1 NOT IN (SELECT c1 FROM t1);   -- returns 9999, expected no rows

Three things have to line up. The subquery must contain a NULL: with a NULL-free
subquery the same query is answered correctly, so the range check is only wrong
in the presence of a NULL. The outer table needs four or more rows, which is
what moves the plan from DEPENDENT SUBQUERY to MATERIALIZED; at three rows the
answer is correct. And the returned value must be unrepresentable in the
subquery column's type - 9999 against TINYINT reproduces, 127 does not.

The defect sits in the partial-matching path for a materialized NOT IN. Setting
materialization=off, or partial_match_rowid_merge=off with
partial_match_table_scan=off, gives the correct answer under either switch.

Both the single-column and the two-column row-constructor form are affected, so
it reaches the code path MDEV-3509 and MDEV-3033 covered. Those two are closed
and fixed, their own testcases give correct results on 13.1.0, and both went the
other way (dropping rows that should have qualified), so this is not a
re-emergence of either.

Affects CS 10.11 through 13.1 and ES 10.6 through 12.3, opt and dbg, on every
engine tested (InnoDB, MyISAM, Aria, MEMORY). Verified by a 30-build sweep of
the reduced testcase; all 20 MariaDB builds differ and all 10 MySQL builds agree
with the reference.

The entry is the exact testcase id and UID from /data/3689720/bug324.report. The
mechanism field carries the row counts of that one trial (rows0v26), so a
different shape of the same defect reports as a finding that sits beside this
entry rather than being muted outright - which is wanted, since a NOT IN
wrong-result on another path still needs looking at.

791a7ad99edf//RESULT|content|SPATIAL|SELECT ST_ASTEXT(ST_UNION(ST_GEOMFROMTEXT('X'), ST_GEOMFROMTEXT('Y')))
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MDEV-36166, "support for notation with brackets inside MULTIPOINT". MariaDB is
the incorrect side. The OGC form of a MULTIPOINT puts each point in its own
brackets. MariaDB reads only the bare form, and rejects the bracketed one:

  SELECT ST_ASTEXT(ST_GEOMFROMTEXT('MULTIPOINT((1 1),(2 2))'));   -- NULL
  SELECT ST_ASTEXT(ST_GEOMFROMTEXT('MULTIPOINT(1 1,2 2)'));       -- MULTIPOINT(1 1,2 2)

The output side is what corlogic sees. ST_ASTEXT prints the bare form, so any
statement building a MULTIPOINT differs from MySQL, which prints the bracketed
one. ST_UNION of two POINTs is the shape the run turned up.

The input side is the real defect. There is no error and no warning, so an
INSERT of the bracketed form stores NULL and nothing is reported. MySQL gives
ERROR 3037 when it cannot read the WKT.

Wider than the ticket's description: ST_MPOINTFROMTEXT does not take the
bracketed form either, and a MULTIPOINT nested in a GEOMETRYCOLLECTION returns
NULL as well. MULTILINESTRING and MULTIPOLYGON both read their nested brackets
correctly, so this is specific to MULTIPOINT.

MySQL added the support in 5.7. MySQL 5.5 and 5.6 reject the bracketed form the
same way MariaDB does, and 5.7, 8.0 and 9.1 all read it. A sweep of the 30
gendirs.sh builds has all 20 MariaDB builds affected, both flavours, CS 10.11
through 13.1 and ES 10.6 through 12.3.

The entry is the exact testcase id and UID from /data/3689720/bug549.report.
Muting the output difference is right because the ticket covers it, but note it
mutes only this statement shape - another statement that builds a MULTIPOINT
reports as a finding sitting beside this entry.

RESULT|content|*|*CONV(*   and   RESULT|content|*|*x'*
------------------------------------------------------
(these live in corlogic.known.mysql)

Hexadecimal and binary string literals. MariaDB is the correct side and this is not a
defect. MariaDB separates the two syntaxes on purpose:

  X'value' and x'value'   the SQL Standard binary string literal, a string in EVERY
                          context, never a number
  0xvalue                 the MySQL/MariaDB hex hybrid, a number in a numeric context

MySQL treats both as numbers, so any statement mixing a literal with arithmetic, a
bitwise operator, a comparison, CAST or CONV can differ. What the run turned up:

  SELECT x'1F' ^ 0x10;            CS gives 16,       MS gives 15
  SELECT CONV(0xFF,16,2);         CS gives 0,        MS gives 11111111
  SELECT CAST(x'aa' AS UNSIGNED); CS gives 0,        MS gives 170

MariaDB warns each time - "Truncated incorrect DOUBLE value: '\x1F'" - so nothing is
silent. The two forms are byte-identical (HEX and LENGTH agree, and x'1F' = 0x1F is
true), because that comparison is a string comparison; only the numeric conversion
differs, which is the documented rule.

The change was made in 5.5.31, intentionally, to fix MDEV-4489 and to follow the SQL
standard. It is documented on the Hexadecimal Literals KB page and on the CAST page.
Closed as expected behaviour three times: MDEV-6092 (Not a Bug), MDEV-10479 (the
documentation was what got fixed) and MDEV-38434 (Not a Bug, 2025, on 11.8).

On CONV specifically, MariaDB is the more coherent of the two. It stringifies the first
argument and reads it in from_base, so CONV('FF',16,2) is 11111111 and CONV(255,16,2) is
1001010101. MySQL returns 11111111 for CONV(0xFF,16,2) AND for CONV(0xFF,10,2), so it
ignores from_base entirely once the argument is a hex literal.

Muted on both patterns because the family reappears through any statement shape the
generator builds around a literal. It cost 5 reports in one run.

ERROR|1055v0|*ORDERBY*|*   and   ERROR|0v1140|*ORDERBY*|*
---------------------------------------------------------

MDEV-27310, "ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY doesn't prevent ORDER BY from using a column
missing in GROUP BY". Open, and it reproduces on every current version.

MariaDB applies ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY to the select list and to HAVING, but not to
the ORDER BY list, so this is accepted where MySQL 5.6 and later refuse it:

  SET sql_mode='ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY';
  CREATE TABLE t (a INT, b INT, c INT);
  SELECT a, MIN(b) FROM t GROUP BY a ORDER BY c, a;

The second pattern is the opposite case, an aggregate with no GROUP BY. There
MariaDB refuses the ORDER BY and MySQL accepts it:

  SELECT SUM(a) FROM t ORDER BY b;

MariaDB gives ERROR 1140, whose text is about mixing group and non-group columns
in the select list. The select list holds only the aggregate, and the text names
neither ORDER BY nor the sql_mode. Both forms sit on the same ticket, because
both come from the same check.

Measured against MS 9.1.0: every MariaDB version accepts the first form, MySQL
5.5 accepts it and MySQL 5.6 onwards refuses it. A same-vendor run never sees
either form.

f05f2e49fd1f//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT GREATEST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM tX WHERE ABS(cX) <N
58b12e6fc964//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT LEAST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM t WHERE ABS(cX) <N
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(these live in corlogic.known.mysql)

GREATEST and LEAST given both a string and a number. MariaDB compares the
arguments as numbers, MySQL compares them as strings, so the two pick different
arguments:

  statement              CS 13.1.0   MS 9.1.0   PostgreSQL 17.11   Oracle 23ai
  GREATEST('1000',295)   1000        295        1000 (integer)     295
  LEAST('1000',295)      295         1000       295                1000
  LEAST('d',295)         0           295        error              295

PostgreSQL agrees with MariaDB and Oracle takes the type of its first argument,
so there is no rule that makes the first two rows anything but the dialect.

The two statements these entries name are the same shape twice, GREATEST and
LEAST over an ENUM column beside an INT column and a number:

  CREATE TABLE t (c1 INT, c2 ENUM('a','b','c','d'));
  INSERT INTO t VALUES(4,'a'),(5,'b'),(6,'c');
  SELECT LEAST(c1,c2,5) FROM t;

  CS 13.1.0   1, 2, 3        the ENUM read as its index, so the ENUM wins
  MS 9.1.0    4, 5, 5        every argument read as a string

Each answer is one of the arguments under that side's own rule.

The third row of the table above is not the dialect. MDEV-40982, "GREATEST/LEAST
mixed string+bigint loses precision", Confirmed: every argument goes through a
double conversion and the conversion is what comes back, so LEAST('d',295)
returns a value no argument held.

That is why these entries name two exact statements instead of the whole
function. An entry on the function would mute that finding as well: the run that
produced these two shapes also produced GREATEST('d',295), which is the MDEV-40982
shape and has to keep reporting. The price is that a mixed-type GREATEST or LEAST
in a shape nobody has judged still costs a report, which is the point of it.


424985bb422a//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT WEIGHT_STRING(cX) AS r0 FROM tX
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
(this lives in corlogic.known.mysql)

WEIGHT_STRING() on a temporal value. MariaDB returns the bytes of the string
form, MySQL returns the packed sort key:

  SELECT HEX(WEIGHT_STRING(DATE'2020-01-01'));

  CS 13.1.0   323032302D30312D3031    the ASCII of '2020-01-01'
  MS 9.1.0    99A5420000000000        the packed key

Over a string column both return the same collation weights, because every side
creates its database CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci, so the
column is on one collation everywhere. Only a temporal argument differs. MySQL
documents the function as one for internal use. The entry names the one
statement shape that was judged, so the function reached another way still
reports.


Cases carried in the code, with no entry here
=============================================

Five cross-vendor families are handled where they arise rather than muted, so
nothing about them sits in corlogic.known.mysql.

ALGORITHM= and LOCK= on an ALTER. ALGORITHM=NOCOPY is a MariaDB keyword and
MySQL answers ERROR 1800 for it. ALGORITHM=COPY with LOCK=NONE is a capability
rather than a keyword, and where it stands on ALTER TABLE t1 ADD c INT:

  ES 10.6.28-24, CS 10.11.19   ERROR 1846
  CS 11.4.13, CS 13.1.0        accepted
  MS 9.1.0                     ERROR 1846

strip_online_ddl() takes both clauses out of the stream on a run whose sides
differ by engine or by vendor. The ALTER still runs on every side, so what it
does to the table is still compared.

The row count an ALTER reports. The count is the rows the statement copied, so a
side that runs the ALTER instantly reports none where a side that rebuilds the
table reports every row it moved. Forcing ALGORITHM=COPY on 13.1.0 gives the
MySQL count, and both tables hold the same data either way. compare_pair()
leaves the count of a DDL alone across the engine axis and across vendors.

A plan difference the server itself explains. Where the two sides of a lookup
are different types MariaDB declines the index and names the reason:

  Note 1105  Cannot use key `k1` part[0] for lookup: `test`.`t1`.`c1` of type
             `int` = "`o`.`c4`" of type `datetime`

MySQL takes the lookup after casting both sides to double, which its own Note
1003 spells out, and both return the same rows. explain_plan() reads the note
and the plan compare drops the difference on a cross-vendor run where one side
declined a key and the other used one. Between builds of one vendor the same
note is a real change and still reports.

An aggregate over an ENUM. SUM(DISTINCT) and AVG(DISTINCT) over an ENUM give
1 and 1.0000 on MariaDB against 1 and 1 on MySQL: MariaDB reads the ENUM as its
numeric index and answers in DECIMAL, MySQL reads it as a string and answers in
DOUBLE. The value is the same. num_policy_match() already treats two renderings
of one number as one value on a cross-vendor run.

A fractional second going into a temporal that carries no sub-second precision.
MariaDB cuts it and MySQL rounds it:

  statement                                   CS 13.1.0             MS 9.1.0
  CAST(35.5 AS TIME)                          00:00:35              00:00:36
  CAST('2020-01-01 10:00:00.5' AS DATETIME)   2020-01-01 10:00:00   2020-01-01 10:00:01

A plain INSERT of the same values into a TIME column and a DATETIME column
splits the same way, so the class is not about CAST. Where each product stands,
on CAST(35.5 AS TIME):

  ES 10.6, CS 13.1   00:00:35, cut
  MS 5.5             00:00:35.500000, the fraction kept
  MS 5.6, MS 9.1     00:00:36, rounded
  PostgreSQL 17.11   rounds
  Oracle 23ai        rounds to TIMESTAMP(0), cuts to DATE

Cutting it is the MariaDB default by design. MDEV-16991, closed as fixed in
10.4.1, added sql_mode=TIME_ROUND_FRACTIONAL for the other behaviour, and on
13.1.0 that flag gives the MySQL answer for both a CAST and a plain INSERT.
Setting the flag on the MariaDB side alone would take the class out at the
source, and it is not done. MySQL refuses the value - "Variable 'sql_mode' can't
be set to the value of 'TIME_ROUND_FRACTIONAL'" - so it cannot be one statement
both sides take, and corlogic only emits an in-band setup statement every side
accepts. The reason is the testcase: a report carries one SET sql_mode line, so
a mode held by one side alone would not replay what the run did.

temporal_round_match() reads it off the cells instead. Where one vendor sits on
each end of the run, resolve_compare_modes() records which side cuts, and a cell
pair that is one temporal value on that side and the second after it on the
other is one value. Everything else about the statement still compares, so a
second expression in the same SELECT that really does differ still carries the
statement to a report.

Reading the cells rather than the statement is what makes this safe. The
generator often pairs two expressions in one SELECT, and an entry on the CAST
text muted about forty shapes in a 3000-trial run. Taking the entry out and
reading the cells turned thirty of those shapes into reports, and they carry one
class the entry had been hiding: a value outside the TIME range, where MySQL
answers NULL and MariaDB clamps.

  SELECT CAST(1e308 AS TIME);

  CS 13.1.0   838:59:59
  MS 9.1.0    NULL

A cell pair two seconds apart, a cell that kept its fraction, a bare DATE, and
any run that is not one vendor against the other are all outside the rule and
stay a difference.


3962a83db959//ERROR|0v1140|AGG|SELECT COUNT(*),cX FROM tX WHERE cX=N AND cX IS NOT NULL
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MDEV-11588, "Support for ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY functional dependency". Stalled.

MySQL 5.7 and later work out that a column the WHERE clause fixes to one value is
functionally dependent on nothing, so a non-aggregated reference to it is allowed.
MariaDB has no such step and refuses the statement:

  SET sql_mode='ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY';
  SELECT COUNT(*),b FROM t WHERE b=2;

  CS 13.1.0  ERROR 1140
  MS 9.1.0   1 row

Without the WHERE clause both sides refuse it, so the difference is the
functional-dependency step alone. This entry names the exact statement, so a
variant is still reported and carries the MDEV-11588 notice.
